ention to Control Condition Changes\n- The ISR `svp_simple_618_001_isr_1` modifies `g` by setting it to `5` and then back to `0`. These modifications can occur at any time if the ISR is enabled and triggered.\n- In the main function, the assertions at lines 17 and 19 assume that `g` remains `0`. However, if the ISR preempts the main function between these two assertions, the value of `g` could temporarily change to `5` and then back to `0`, leading to potential atomicity violations.\n\n---\n\n### Step 3: Check Interrupt Status\n- The code does not explicitly disable or enable interrupts. Therefore, we assume that interrupts are enabled by default unless stated otherwise.\n- Since the ISR can preempt the main function at any time, the read operations at lines 17 and 19 in the main function can be interleaved with the write operations at lines 9 and 11 in the ISR.\n\n---\n\n### Step 4: Generate the Final Report\n\n#### Summary of Analysis\n- **Line 17 (`Read`)**: Always executed because it is part of the main function's sequential flow.\n- **Line 19 (`Read`)**: Always executed because it is part of the main function's sequential flow.\n- **Line 9 (`Write`)**: Always executed when the ISR is triggered.\n- **Line 11 (`Write`)**: Always executed when the ISR is triggered.\n\nAll operations on `g` are accessible and can be interleaved due to the lack of synchronization mechanisms. The reported atomicity violations are valid because the ISR can preempt the main function between the two assertions, leading to inconsistent reads.\n\n---\n\n### Final Output\n```json\n{\n    \"violations\": [\n        {\n            \"sharedVariable\": \"g\",\n            \"accessPattern\": [\"Read\", \"Write\", \"Read\"],\n            \"lines\": [17, 9, 19]\n        },\n        {\n            \"sharedVariable\": \"g\",\n            \"accessPattern\": [\"Read\", \"Write\", \"Read\"],\n            \"lines\": [17, 11, 19]\n        }\n    ]\n}\n```\n\n[Expert R2] (Refine the report based on judge